WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:02.601
-Welcome back to "Late Night."
We're here with Chris Hayes.

00:00:02.601 --> 00:00:03.803
You wrote a "New York Times"
essay.

00:00:03.803 --> 00:00:06.205
-I did.
-Making the case for why you

00:00:06.205 --> 00:00:08.074
wanted Twitter to survive.

00:00:08.074 --> 00:00:09.809
I think that this is a time
where a lot of people

00:00:09.809 --> 00:00:11.844
talk about
what a cesspool it is.

00:00:11.844 --> 00:00:14.780
I liked reading about the fact

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:16.682
that it has also been
a wonderful place.

00:00:16.682 --> 00:00:18.084
-Yeah, there's lots of things
that have been

00:00:18.084 --> 00:00:19.218
really remarkable about it.

00:00:19.218 --> 00:00:21.420
I mean, one of the things
I think --

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:24.223
The thing that was best about it
is, because of how

00:00:24.223 --> 00:00:28.294
it was structured, in which you
weren't necessarily just in one,

00:00:28.294 --> 00:00:29.729
you know, one part of Reddit,
right,

00:00:29.729 --> 00:00:31.397
like, "Oh, I'm an electric car
enthusiast,"

00:00:31.397 --> 00:00:33.799
I'm on, like, the Reddit board,
you had this sort of general

00:00:33.799 --> 00:00:35.367
feed, and because of the way
retweets worked

00:00:35.367 --> 00:00:37.803
and the algorithm,
you would happen upon stuff.

00:00:37.803 --> 00:00:39.405
You know?
And so for me as a --

00:00:39.405 --> 00:00:42.608
particularly as a news gatherer,
you know, there'd be

00:00:42.608 --> 00:00:44.477
an impending railroad strike,
and then all of a sudden

00:00:44.477 --> 00:00:46.112
I would have like three or four
people that work

00:00:46.112 --> 00:00:49.582
on the railroad talking about,
like, "This is why we want

00:00:49.582 --> 00:00:52.985
paid leave," and this is why
this new system they use

00:00:52.985 --> 00:00:55.422
in the railroads is totally
oppressive and brutal,

00:00:55.422 --> 00:00:57.057
scheduling-wise,
and you would have this

00:00:57.057 --> 00:00:58.825
for a million different
experiences.

00:00:58.825 --> 00:01:00.827
You would get these windows,
and again, that could be

00:01:00.827 --> 00:01:03.395
dangerous because a little
knowledge is a dangerous thing.

00:01:03.395 --> 00:01:05.465
Like, I know about the railroads
now, right?

00:01:05.465 --> 00:01:07.133
-Yeah.
-I followed these three people.

00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:11.504
But I found, for me, the breadth
of experience and perspectives

00:01:11.504 --> 00:01:13.973
that were all mixing in one
place, there really hasn't ever

00:01:13.973 --> 00:01:15.875
been anything quite
to replicate that,

00:01:15.875 --> 00:01:17.810
and I don't want it to go away.

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:21.714
-And what do you think in the
early days of Elon Musk's,

00:01:21.714 --> 00:01:24.717
you know, running of Twitter --
are you -- I mean, again,

00:01:24.717 --> 00:01:26.518
you're not a business guy,
but are you basically thinking,

00:01:26.518 --> 00:01:27.887
"He's nailing it," right?

00:01:27.887 --> 00:01:30.056
-[ Laughter ]
-I mean, it's 10 out of 10.

00:01:30.056 --> 00:01:31.290
As far as I can tell.
-10 out of 10, yeah.

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:32.892
Every part of it.
-I mean, I'm not in the books

00:01:32.892 --> 00:01:34.827
but I think pretty much --
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:34.827 --> 00:01:38.530
-Sounds like pretty much --
No, it's been pretty disastrous.

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:41.734
I mean, I think the thing
that I actually have --

00:01:41.734 --> 00:01:43.803
that has been clarifying for me,
I think there was --

00:01:43.803 --> 00:01:45.905
First of all, it felt a little
bit like an impulse purchase.

00:01:45.905 --> 00:01:47.173
-Yeah.
-[ Laughter ]

00:01:47.173 --> 00:01:49.242
-Like he was at the cash
register and there was like

00:01:49.242 --> 00:01:51.945
a $44 billion
social-media company.

00:01:51.945 --> 00:01:54.080
-[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:56.682
-So that was weird, that he --

00:01:56.682 --> 00:01:59.852
It was the most expensive
impulse purchase of all time.

00:02:00.953 --> 00:02:04.023
I think that what he's been
doing with the platform now is

00:02:04.023 --> 00:02:07.326
actually very clarifying to me
because it wasn't about

00:02:07.326 --> 00:02:08.727
the business and it wasn't
an impulse purchase.

00:02:08.727 --> 00:02:10.230
It was an ideological purchase.

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:12.499
And as long as there have been
rich people,

00:02:12.499 --> 00:02:15.134
and as long as there have been
rich people and capitalism,

00:02:15.134 --> 00:02:18.805
plus a free press, there have
been rich people who buy

00:02:18.805 --> 00:02:22.207
media outlets
for ideological purposes.

00:02:22.207 --> 00:02:24.644
This is a story as old as time.

00:02:24.644 --> 00:02:27.646
And once I sort of saw it
as that, I was like, "Oh!

00:02:27.646 --> 00:02:30.182
That's what this is."

00:02:30.182 --> 00:02:35.088
He wants to own Twitter because
he wants to pursue his vision

00:02:35.088 --> 00:02:36.422
because he's
the world's richest man.

00:02:36.422 --> 00:02:38.724
The other thing I think is key
here is --

00:02:38.724 --> 00:02:40.125
You know, I'm one of
these people who is

00:02:40.125 --> 00:02:43.028
A, a Twitter addict, and B,
it's, like, "partly my job."

00:02:43.028 --> 00:02:44.464
-Yep.
-It's like if you're, like,

00:02:44.464 --> 00:02:47.133
working at RJR Nabisco, and
you're like, smoking two packs

00:02:47.133 --> 00:02:48.768
a day, you're like, "Well, it's
the product. You know?

00:02:48.768 --> 00:02:51.304
I gotta test it."
-[ Laughter ]

00:02:51.304 --> 00:02:53.205
It's like, that's sort of me.

00:02:53.205 --> 00:02:54.573
But that's also Elon Musk.

00:02:54.573 --> 00:02:56.743
I think he bought it so he could
be like, "I'm working."

00:02:56.743 --> 00:02:58.177
-Yeah!
-[ Laughter ]

00:02:58.177 --> 00:02:59.579
That's so smart. Right.

00:02:59.579 --> 00:03:01.514
Like when my wife is like,
"Are you still on Twitter?"

00:03:01.514 --> 00:03:03.683
-Like, "I'm working!"
-"I bought it, honey!"

00:03:03.683 --> 00:03:04.750
-Yeah.
-[ Laughter ]

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:07.486
-I gotta be on it. I bought it.

00:03:07.486 --> 00:03:11.691
Can I just say you crushed
Twitter from the start?

00:03:11.691 --> 00:03:13.826
-Oh, this is amazing.
-We went back and looked.

00:03:13.826 --> 00:03:16.829
And like a lot of us, it took us
time to figure out how to be

00:03:16.829 --> 00:03:19.532
good at Twitter, but we went
back to your first four tweets.

00:03:19.532 --> 00:03:21.934
This is April 12, 2007.

00:03:21.934 --> 00:03:23.502 line:0%
I want to reveal them as we go.

00:03:23.502 --> 00:03:26.238 line:0%
So, that's "working on
a presentation in Keynote."

00:03:26.238 --> 00:03:27.240 line:0%
Thank you for that.

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:28.907 line:0%
-[ Laughter ]
-Yep.

00:03:28.907 --> 00:03:30.776 line:0%
One heart right there.
-One heart, right.

00:03:30.776 --> 00:03:32.545 line:0%
-[ Laughter ]
-Hearted it up.

00:03:32.545 --> 00:03:35.114 line:0%
-By the way, like, imagine
retweeting that.

00:03:35.114 --> 00:03:37.650 line:0%
[ Laughter ]

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:39.051 line:0%
Like imagine someone being like,

00:03:39.051 --> 00:03:40.519 line:0%
"Oh, I want my followers
to see that."

00:03:40.519 --> 00:03:41.854 line:0%
Alright, then you
come back with,

00:03:41.854 --> 00:03:44.657 line:0%
"in a coffee shop
working on a draft."

00:03:44.657 --> 00:03:46.726 line:0%
[ Laughter ]

00:03:46.726 --> 00:03:48.861 line:0%
More likes but less retweets.

00:03:48.861 --> 00:03:50.430
-Mm-hmm.

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:55.768
-"Reading blogs
and craving caffeine." Oh!

00:03:55.768 --> 00:03:57.837
-That was a perpetual
state of being.

00:03:57.837 --> 00:04:00.406
-And then this is
maybe the most --

00:04:00.406 --> 00:04:02.475
the flattest tweet
I've ever seen.

00:04:02.475 --> 00:04:04.978
[ Laughter ]

00:04:04.978 --> 00:04:06.511
"Listening to Terry Gross."

00:04:06.511 --> 00:04:09.948
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:04:09.948 --> 00:04:11.884
-Well, at least I'm not --
-Now, I'm -- By the way,

00:04:11.884 --> 00:04:14.152
I listen to Terry Gross.
I love Terry Gross.

00:04:14.152 --> 00:04:16.355
I've never, while I've listened
to Terry Gross, think...

00:04:16.355 --> 00:04:18.057
-Tweeted that?
-..."I gotta let people know."

00:04:18.057 --> 00:04:19.458
[ Laughter ]

00:04:19.458 --> 00:04:22.060
-At least I'm not a caricature
of myself.

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:26.832
I will note, for my -- to defend
my honor here, briefly,

00:04:26.832 --> 00:04:30.035
do you remember back then
the Twitter prompt was,

00:04:30.035 --> 00:04:32.338
"chrislhayes is..."?

00:04:32.338 --> 00:04:33.405
-Ah!
-That was the prompt.

00:04:33.405 --> 00:04:34.407
The prompt was like --

00:04:34.407 --> 00:04:36.208
It was like a status away
message.

00:04:36.208 --> 00:04:39.211
Like you used to have on AIM
back in the day, people our age.

00:04:39.211 --> 00:04:43.116
And so, like, the setup was,
"What are you doing right now?"

00:04:43.116 --> 00:04:45.218
-Tell us what you're actively
doing right now.

00:04:45.218 --> 00:04:47.253
-Exactly. Just, you know --
-Okay, gotcha.

00:04:47.253 --> 00:04:48.654
But I evolved.
I think I got better.

00:04:48.654 --> 00:04:51.590
-You know, listening to
Terry Gross sounded boring.

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:55.594
But just to make it fair, you
should have heard it like this.

00:04:55.594 --> 00:04:57.396
"Chris Hayes is..."

00:04:57.396 --> 00:04:58.798
[ Laughter ]

00:04:58.798 --> 00:05:00.433
"...listening to Terry Gross."

00:05:00.433 --> 00:05:01.433
-[ Laughter ]
-Thank you!

00:05:01.433 --> 00:05:03.302
Right? Better.
-There you go.

00:05:03.302 --> 00:05:06.772
-Better.
-[ Applause ]

00:05:06.772 --> 00:05:09.475
-You know, you tease yourself
for being very online

00:05:09.475 --> 00:05:11.177
and living an online life.
It should be noted,

00:05:11.177 --> 00:05:12.745
another thing that's remarkable,

00:05:12.745 --> 00:05:15.581
you and I are very much inside
that Twitter bubble.

00:05:15.581 --> 00:05:17.750
We think it's the most important
social-media network

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:19.752
in the world,
and it is decisively not.

00:05:19.752 --> 00:05:21.020
-No, definitely not.

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:24.624
And it's never, you know,
delivered a ton of growth.

00:05:24.624 --> 00:05:26.459
It's never been particularly
far-reaching.

00:05:26.459 --> 00:05:28.127
It has always been
a bit of a bubble.

00:05:28.127 --> 00:05:33.165
And again, I also wonder how
much this -- you know, again,

00:05:33.165 --> 00:05:35.534
going back to that point about
you can't extrapolate out some

00:05:35.534 --> 00:05:38.671
trend and -- because there's
disruption and there's changes.

00:05:38.671 --> 00:05:41.974
I don't know if this era we've
lived in basically, I think

00:05:41.974 --> 00:05:44.409
since the introduction
of the iPhone in 2007,

00:05:44.409 --> 00:05:48.381
which is the kind of
fully smartphone, web 2.0,

00:05:48.381 --> 00:05:51.483
social-media universe that we've
lived in now for 15 years.

00:05:51.483 --> 00:05:54.586
I don't know if we're at
the precipice of something new.

00:05:54.586 --> 00:05:58.390
You know? It feels to me like
there's shifts happening.

00:05:58.390 --> 00:06:00.793
I don't know where it goes next,
but I also feel -- There's a

00:06:00.793 --> 00:06:03.996
little bit of an end of an epoch
feeling to me right now.

00:06:03.996 --> 00:06:06.532
-You also do a good job -- If
the the epoch ends and it's more

00:06:06.532 --> 00:06:09.202
outdoorsy, you're also
a very outdoorsy fella.

00:06:09.202 --> 00:06:11.537
-I know.
-You clear some brush.

00:06:11.537 --> 00:06:13.906
-The silence in the crowd is
the surprise registering.

00:06:13.906 --> 00:06:15.475
-[ Laughter ]
-But you did something.

00:06:15.475 --> 00:06:18.744
You posted this photo of you and
your wife on your anniversary.

00:06:18.744 --> 00:06:22.547
And this is last July.
-This is our 15th anniversary.

00:06:22.547 --> 00:06:24.817
-Your 15th anniversary.
And where did you take her?

00:06:24.817 --> 00:06:26.252
-The garbage dump.
-The garbage dump.

00:06:26.252 --> 00:06:27.886
[ Laughter ]

00:06:27.886 --> 00:06:30.055
And I will say the fact that --

00:06:30.055 --> 00:06:31.623
I mean, I do think
that's a good test.

00:06:31.623 --> 00:06:34.393
At 15 years, if you can make out
in front of a pile of garbage --

00:06:34.393 --> 00:06:35.461
[ Laughter ]

00:06:35.461 --> 00:06:37.496
-And in my -- I just want
to be clear here.

00:06:37.496 --> 00:06:38.564
This was not unilateral.

00:06:38.564 --> 00:06:40.699
This was a --
We were both psyched.

00:06:40.699 --> 00:06:43.402
-Okay, that's nice.
-Together to go --

00:06:43.402 --> 00:06:44.570
Kate's parents were in town.

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:46.004
They nicely spelled us
for a little bit.

00:06:46.004 --> 00:06:47.440
We had a lot of garbage
piling up.

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:50.275
We headed down to the dump.
We were like, "Let's together,

00:06:50.275 --> 00:06:52.812
after 15 years, cross this item
off our to-do list."

00:06:52.812 --> 00:06:54.080
-That's really sweet.
-Together.

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:55.714
I think that is --
That's the foundation of love.

00:06:55.714 --> 00:06:57.683
-Chris Hayes is
finding true love.

00:06:57.683 --> 00:07:00.319
-Yes, under a garbage dump.
-Under a garbage dump.

00:07:00.319 --> 00:07:01.720
Hey, thanks for being here.
-Thank you, man.

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:03.355
-We're looking forward to
following you tonight.

00:07:03.355 --> 00:07:04.724
You guys, that's Chris Hayes.

00:07:04.724 --> 00:07:07.526
"All In with Chris Hayes,"
weeknights at 8:00 on MSNBC.

00:07:07.526 --> 00:07:08.961
Tune in for the network's

00:07:08.961 --> 00:07:10.496
continuing coverage of
the Georgia election.

00:07:10.496 --> 00:07:12.198 line:0%
We'll be right back with
Charlotte Nicdao.

